  "answer": "Ohm’s law states that the potential difference (V) across the ends of a conductor is directly proportional to the electric current (I) passing through it, provided the temperature and other physical conditions remain constant. Mathematically, it is expressed as V = IR, where R is the resistance of the conductor.\n\nTo verify Ohm’s law, an experiment can be conducted as follows: Set up a circuit with a battery, a resistor (such as a bulb), an ammeter, and a voltmeter. Connect the ammeter in series with the resistor and the voltmeter in parallel across the resistor. Adjust the battery voltage by using multiple cells in series and record the corresponding current and potential difference values. Calculate the ratio V/I for each set of values. If the ratio remains constant, it confirms that Ohm’s law holds true for the conductor used. A graph of V versus I should be a straight line passing through the origin, indicating a direct proportionality between voltage and current.",
